{"metadata": {"team_id": "uva", "run_id": "mq4cs-gpt41-bm25", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_8", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Taking it slow and giving your finger time to heal is a wise choice. When you start climbing again, focus on a gradual and controlled approach to rebuilding finger strength. Start with putty exercises to help with finger flexibility and strength. Use manual resistance, employing your other hand to provide light resistance, which helps isolate finger muscles without overloading them. Incorporate rubber band exercises to further enhance finger strength.  When you're ready to return to climbing, consider deloaded hangboard work. Use a pulley system or assist to lighten the load on your fingers while practicing grip positions. This helps condition them without excessive stress. Begin with light finger curling exercises using a small dumbbell to gradually add resistance back to your routine.  Above all, avoid rushing or pushing through pain as it can lead to reinjury. Listen to your body and pay attention to any signs of discomfort. Consistent, gentle training focused on increasing resistance gradually will be key to regaining full capacity in your fingers. Taking your time will help ensure a smoother and safer transition back into climbing.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:7": 4.1862390960318905, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 4.132388134540984, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 4.020149047482215, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:3": 3.918702419307937, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:15": 3.4268919482649567, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:8": 3.412991421912141, "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:0": 3.241491814551299, "clueweb22-en0026-20-01553:17": 3.12373909810049, "clueweb22-en0044-90-18231:0": 3.1074873904298617, "clueweb22-en0006-07-05326:2": 3.0742834783830535}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.", "I climb outdoors."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:7": 4.1862390960318905, "clueweb22-en0007-15-